## The idea

Turn every known word into its **keypad number** once ("careers" → 2273377), and store a dictionary from number → list of words. Looking up a phone number is then a single dictionary lookup. Generating every letter combination of the number would be exponential — looking it up is not. For the follow-up (suggest words while the user is still typing), store the same numbers in a **trie over digits**, and collect every word below the prefix node.

## Step by step

1. Map each letter to its digit: abc → 2, def → 3, …, wxyz → 9.
2. For every known word, build its digit string ("signature"); skip words with non-letters.
3. Store signature → list of words (several words can share one signature).
4. WordsFor(number) returns the list for that exact number, or an empty list.
5. Trie: one node per digit. Add(word) walks/creates nodes along the signature and stores the word at the end node.
6. WithPrefix(digits): walk to the node for the digits, then collect every word stored in that node and all nodes below it.

## Worked example

**Input:** known words: careers, linkedin, linkedgo; number 54653346

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| careers | c a r e e r s → 2 2 7 3 3 7 7 → "2273377" |
| linkedin | l i n k e d i n → 5 4 6 5 3 3 4 6 → "54653346" |
| linkedgo | l i n k e d g o → 5 4 6 5 3 3 4 6 → "54653346" (same!) |
| WordsFor("54653346") | dictionary lookup → [linkedin, linkedgo] |

**Result:** [linkedin, linkedgo]

## Cost

Building: O(total letters). WordsFor: O(length of the number). Trie prefix search: O(prefix length + number of matches). Memory O(total letters).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public class KeypadDictionary {
    // signature (digits) -> every word that types to it
    private readonly Dictionary<string, List<string>> _bySignature = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>();

    public KeypadDictionary(IEnumerable<string> words) {
        foreach (string word in words) {
            string signature = Signature(word);
            if (signature == null) continue;                    // has a non-letter: cannot be typed
            if (!_bySignature.ContainsKey(signature)) _bySignature[signature] = new List<string>();
            _bySignature[signature].Add(word);
        }
    }

    // "careers" -> "2273377". Returns null if the word has a character that is not a letter.
    public static string Signature(string word) {
        string[] groups = { "abc", "def", "ghi", "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz" };   // keys 2..9
        System.Text.StringBuilder digits = new System.Text.StringBuilder();
        foreach (char raw in word) {
            char c = char.ToLowerInvariant(raw);
            int digit = -1;
            for (int g = 0; g < groups.Length; g++) {
                if (groups[g].IndexOf(c) >= 0) { digit = g + 2; break; }
            }
            if (digit == -1) return null;
            digits.Append(digit);
        }
        return digits.ToString();
    }

    public List<string> WordsFor(string number) {
        List<string> words;
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(number) || !_bySignature.TryGetValue(number, out words)) return new List<string>();
        return new List<string>(words);                           // a copy, so callers cannot change ours
    }
}

// Follow-up: suggest words while the user is still typing digits.
public class KeypadTrie {
    private class Node {
        public Node[] Next = new Node[10];                        // one child per digit 0..9
        public List<string> Words = new List<string>();           // words that END at this node
    }

    private readonly Node _root = new Node();

    public void Add(string word) {
        string signature = KeypadDictionary.Signature(word);
        if (signature == null) return;
        Node current = _root;
        foreach (char d in signature) {
            int digit = d - '0';
            if (current.Next[digit] == null) current.Next[digit] = new Node();
            current = current.Next[digit];
        }
        current.Words.Add(word);
    }

    public List<string> Exact(string number) {
        Node node = Walk(number);
        if (node == null) return new List<string>();
        return new List<string>(node.Words);
    }

    public List<string> WithPrefix(string digits) {
        List<string> found = new List<string>();
        Node node = Walk(digits);
        if (node != null) Collect(node, found);
        return found;
    }

    // Follow the digits from the root. null if the path does not exist (or a digit is 0/1).
    private Node Walk(string digits) {
        Node current = _root;
        foreach (char d in digits) {
            if (d < '2' || d > '9') return null;
            current = current.Next[d - '0'];
            if (current == null) return null;
        }
        return current;
    }

    private static void Collect(Node node, List<string> into) {
        into.AddRange(node.Words);
        foreach (Node child in node.Next) {
            if (child != null) Collect(child, into);
        }
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Digits 0 and 1? Upper-case words? Empty number?**

No letters on 0/1 → no match; normalise case; empty → none

**Q: Lookup cost?**

O(L) after O(total letters) preprocessing

**Q: Words are added while lookups run.**

Copy-on-write the dictionary, or a reader/writer lock — reads dominate

**Q: A million known words.**

Same map; memory is the words themselves

**Q: Suggest as the user types.**

A trie on digit signatures

**Q: How would you test it?**

Signature table for every letter, including s, v, y, z

